 "facts": [
  {
   "text": "Anthropic's restricted model, Claude Mythos Preview, found a 27-year-old bug in OpenBSD, among thousands of high-severity vulnerabilities it says it found.",
   "source": "Anthropic; The Register",
   "on": 248.56,
   "off": 256.924
  },
  {
   "text": "Access went to a limited group of partners through Project Glasswing, with up to $100M in usage credits.",
   "source": "Anthropic; The Register",
   "on": 257.524,
   "off": 264.978
  },
  {
   "text": "By October 2, 2026, Anthropic counted 29,439 candidate findings: 6,157 disclosed, 516 patched.",
   "source": "Anthropic disclosure dashboard",
   "on": 265.578,
   "off": 271.518
  },
  {
   "text": "Anthropic says the limit is now how fast bugs can be verified, disclosed and patched.",
   "source": "Anthropic",
   "on": 272.118,
   "off": 278.663
  },
  {
   "text": "Mozilla found and fixed 271 vulnerabilities in a single Firefox release while testing the model.",
   "source": "Anthropic",
   "on": 279.263,
   "off": 285.808
  },
  {
   "text": "Node.js maintainers report “an incredible amount of AI slop” in vulnerability reports.",
   "source": "HeroDevs",
   "on": 286.408,
   "off": 292.045
  },
  {
   "text": "In August 2026, researchers using OpenAI and Google models found PHP2Shell, flaws in the language behind about 70% of websites whose server-side language is known.",
   "source": "Digital Watch; CNN",
   "on": 292.645,
   "off": 301.145
  },
  {
   "text": "When the fix shipped on October 21, sites went offline worldwide while operators patched.",
   "source": "CNN; Digital Watch",
   "on": 301.745,
   "off": 307.987
  },
  {
   "text": "Anthropic's stated reason for withholding: without safeguards, such models would make exploiting flawed software cheaper for almost anyone.",
   "source": "SecurityAffairs",
   "on": 308.587,
   "off": 316.042
  },
  {
   "text": "Critics call the arrangement “a private club.”",
   "source": "SecurityPal, citing Georgia Tech's Internet Governance Project",
   "on": 316.642,
   "off": 320.763
  },
  {
   "text": "Mythos Preview's successor is the same underlying model as Claude Fable, which Anthropic sells publicly with added safeguards.",
   "source": "Anthropic",
   "on": 321.363,
   "off": 328.818
  }
 ],
 "bookend": {
  "on": 329.818,
  "scroll_s": 30.0,
  "last_s": 4.5,
  "last": "Check every word with a voice from away.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "title": "Audit 1: Gemini",
    "md": "**Audit log**\n\n| Finding | Gemini's claim | Checked against | Resolution |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| \"Read the whole kernel\" | Overstated; models work agentically, not in one read | Fair. The 27-year bug was also in OpenBSD, not \"the kernel\" in general | Took its \"ghosts\" line. Dropped its \"an hour vs. weeks\" line, which no source supports for this model |\n| My bridge annotation | Self-contradictory | Correct, it was garbled. Anthropic's stated argument: a similarly capable model released without safeguards would make exploitation cheaper for almost anyone | Accepted. The lyric's logic was fine; the note was wrong |\n| Verse 4 as inoculation | A graceful confession makes the lab look uniquely honest | Fair, and the verse was my idea | Rewritten as a plain fact plus an instruction to check it, with no charm. The verse now also covers something neither draft caught: a safeguarded version of the same model, Fable 5.1, is sold publicly, while the full Mythos tier stays gated |\n| Maintainers missing | The song sidelines the unpaid people doing the fixes | A real gap | Adopted its basement verse nearly as written |\n| \"Paying guests,\" \"rent,\" \"VIP pass\" | Access is sold | Glasswing came with Anthropic committing up to $100M in usage credits. I found no report of partners paying | Rejected. \"Chosen guests\" and \"some get a pass\" keep the private-club critique accurate |\n| \"Monopoly\" | — | Inaccurate. Another lab's model found PHP2Shell | Rejected. The moat argument stays as a chorus variant, voiced as the lab's satirical aside; the fact card should attribute it to critics |\n| \"Half the world's websites\" | Overstated | Fair. Reports put PHP behind about 70% of websites whose server-side language is known, but nobody knows the unpatched share | Changed to \"nobody knows who's not patched\" |\n| \"Margins are steady\" | — | Unsupported | Changed to \"funding is steady\" |\n| Names look \"hypothetical\" | — | Gemini had no sources pasted, as in the last audit. All three are documented this week or this year | Rejected. Rerun with sources pasted |\n| Auditor's own conflict | — | Google is a Glasswing partner, and Gemini 4 Argon was one of the models used on PHP2Shell | Logged. Its revision never touched the neighbor's crib |"
   },
   {
    "title": "Audit 2: ChatGPT",
    "md": "**Audit log, second pass (ChatGPT)**\n\n| Finding | Claim | Resolution |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| \"Hands aren't steady\" | Implies maintainers are incompetent, when delays are often careful engineering | Accepted. Took its \"ground isn't steady\" |\n| \"Half real and half noise\" | Sounds like a measured 50% false-positive rate | Accepted. Took its \"some signal, some noise\" |\n| \"Billionaires' toys\" | Implies AI findings are useless, rather than criticizing the imbalance | Partly accepted. \"Sent free\" keeps the jab and points it at who bears the cost, without calling the findings worthless |\n| Missing the case against releasing it | The song shows the cost of withholding the model, not the cost of releasing it | Accepted, and it's the most important finding. The bridge now shows the neighbor's discovery taking real sites offline, and \"some locks are for danger, and some are for class\" concedes that some restrictions are genuine. That last line adapts its \"some safeguards are real\" |\n| Contract outro | Fair as a punchline | Kept. Both auditors liked it |\n| Cited sources | A 2026 CISA guidance and a 2026 false-positive study | Not verified by me. Check them before anything from them reaches the fact card |\n| Named events \"hypothetical\" | Same as Gemini | Rejected. Neither auditor had sources pasted |\n| Auditor's own conflict | Not mentioned | OpenAI led the PHP2Shell coalition, and its model is the neighbor's kid. Its rewrite softened that line to \"a PHP page.\" Logged, not assumed to be bias |"
   },
   {
    "title": "Audit 3: GLM",
    "md": "**Where it's wrong**\n- **\"Twenty-seven-year ghosts\" is not a hallucination.** It's Mythos Preview's 27-year-old OpenBSD bug, reported by The Register and in Glasswing launch coverage. GLM couldn't find a source, treated that as evidence the claim was false, and then said the number had \"survived three models.\" That's the exact failure the song is about, and worth a line in the audit log.\n- **\"The town killed its lights\" is documented.** It's PHP2Shell this week: government sites worldwide went offline, and the NSA told PHP users to patch or take their systems offline.\n- **Its referent table is mostly misidentified.** It read the song as Google's Big Sleep and SQLite, the glass room as generic sandboxing, and the guests as Big Sleep's early-access program. The song's actual subjects are Glasswing, PHP2Shell and Fable.\n\n**Where it's right**\n- **The \"billionaires' toys\" line blurs two different actors.** The flood of junk reports comes mostly from bounty hunters using consumer models, not from the labs' own research agents. That makes the line sharper, not weaker: the hunters are using the labs' products.\n- **\"For nothing a year\" is overbroad.** ChatGPT flagged it too, and some maintainers are paid. Two auditors agreeing is enough to soften it.\n- **Verse 4 needs something checkable.** That's fair, and it's better fixed in the video than in the lyric: during verse 4, show the real public model page on screen, so the claim can be checked against a source."
   }
